Java does not have a built-in Date class, but we can import thejava.timepackage to work with the date and time API. The package includes many date and time classes. 
For example:

To display the current date, import thejava.time.LocalDateclass, and use itsnow()method:

To display the current time (hour, minute, second, and nanoseconds), import thejava.time.LocalTimeclass, and use itsnow()method:

To display the current date and time, import thejava.time.LocalDateTimeclass, and use itsnow()method:

The "T" in the example above is used to separate the date from the time. You can use theDateTimeFormatterclass 
with theofPattern()method in the same package to format or parse date-time objects. 
The following example will remove both the "T" and nanoseconds from the date-time:

TheofPattern()method accepts all sorts of values, if you want to display 
the date and time in a different format. For example:

